{
  "name": "reparenting under a descendant is refused as a loop even when a rank re-definition made it rank-legal",
  "notes": "The rank re-definition is the point. Types are events and the last definition wins, so moving `phase` from rank 1 to rank 3 puts it BELOW the `step` it already contains — after which the loop-closing reparent is rank-legal and nothing but the ancestor walk refuses it. Without the second definition this fixture would be caught by the rank check and would prove nothing about cycles. The re-definition is a PRIOR, so it is never itself evaluated: priorEvents are the accepted past.",
